# 5.13.1
sub1("A single global threshold makes it worse — a joint rule requiring 75% sensitivity everywhere lands at 0.286 and keeps 18.5% worst-case specificity, and in 48.2% of bootstrap resamples at an 0.85 floor no threshold satisfies the floor everywhere — while the cohorts allowed to choose alone at a 0.75 floor pick 0.510, 0.291 and 0.607, a spread of 0.32 that is structural rather than noise. Per-deployment threshold setting on local data is therefore a requirement of the method, not a refinement of it.",
     "A single global threshold makes it worse — a joint rule requiring 75% sensitivity everywhere lands at 0.286 and keeps 18.5% worst-case specificity, and in 48.2% of bootstrap resamples at an 0.85 floor no threshold satisfies the floor everywhere — while the cohorts allowed to choose alone at a 0.75 floor pick 0.510, 0.291 and 0.607, a structural spread of 0.32. Per-deployment threshold setting on local data is therefore a requirement of the method, not a refinement.")
sub1("A single logistic recalibration fitted on the pooled development predictions (slope 1.2887, intercept 0.1376; the external corpus played no part in the fit) improves Pittsburgh (slope 1.516 to 1.176), worsens Delaware on the very data the map was fitted on (0.823 to 0.638, Brier 0.220 to 0.222), and on the external corpus improves the slope (1.886 to 1.464) while worsening the intercept and the Brier score (0.179 to 0.182); the mechanism is section 5.4.1's finding again, two development cohorts miscalibrated in opposite directions, and discrimination is unchanged everywhere, as a monotone transformation requires (section 3.11). Both the threshold and the calibration map are local quantities, which is the empirical case for the staged deployment design of Chapter 6. Rules that read only the target cohort's unlabelled score distribution — percentile, mean-matching, z-normalisation, quantile mapping — are diagnostics, not candidates: mean-matching collapses exactly where prevalence differs most (transported from Pittsburgh to Delaware at a 90% sensitivity target it lands at 99.4% sensitivity and 1.1% specificity, the worst cell in the grid), because it treats a prevalence difference as a calibration difference; and a percentile rule is arithmetically identical to fixing the referral rate, which transported acceptably here only because the external cohort's prevalence sits within two points of development's — in a Libyan community clinic at 5% to 15% prevalence it would refer over half of everyone.",
     "A single logistic recalibration fitted on the pooled development predictions (slope 1.2887, intercept 0.1376; the external corpus played no part) improves Pittsburgh (slope 1.516 to 1.176), worsens Delaware on the very data it was fitted on (0.823 to 0.638, Brier 0.220 to 0.222), and on the external corpus improves the slope (1.886 to 1.464) while worsening the intercept and the Brier score (0.179 to 0.182): section 5.4.1's finding again, two development cohorts miscalibrated in opposite directions, with discrimination unchanged everywhere as a monotone transformation requires (section 3.11). Threshold and calibration map are both local quantities, which is the empirical case for the staged deployment of Chapter 6. Rules that read only the target cohort's unlabelled score distribution — percentile, mean-matching, z-normalisation, quantile mapping — are diagnostics, not candidates: mean-matching treats a prevalence difference as a calibration difference and collapses where prevalence differs most (Pittsburgh to Delaware at a 90% sensitivity target: 99.4% sensitivity and 1.1% specificity, the worst cell in the grid), and a percentile rule is arithmetically a fixed referral rate, which transported acceptably here only because the external cohort's prevalence sits within two points of development's — in a Libyan community clinic at 5% to 15% prevalence it would refer over half of everyone.")

