sub1("Specificity becomes a design parameter that is set, sensitivity becomes the measured, reported quantity, and Delaware's 0.356 is the discrimination problem of section 5.13.2 reappearing in the only place it can, not a failure of the rule. On the external corpus the bootstrap gives specificity 0.774 [0.69, 0.80], strikingly tight, against sensitivity 0.769 [0.44, 0.96], loose because 26 impaired recordings cannot pin it down; the tight interval is an in-sample quantity, since the same 27 controls defined the threshold and were scored against it. The prospective quantity has an exact, distribution-free answer: with the threshold at the k-th order statistic of n controls, prospective specificity follows a Beta(k, n+1−k) law, which for 27 controls gives 0.786 [0.62, 0.91] — the number a clinician actually cares about — and Table (5.17) turns that law into the pilot's sample-size justification: a ±10-point interval needs about 59 healthy speakers, ±7.5 points about 108 (`results/reconstruction/control_threshold_precision.json`). The assumption the pilot will not fully satisfy is stated with its direction: the law assumes the control sample is drawn from the population the threshold will serve, and a healthier-than-typical convenience sample sets the threshold too low, which over-refers; that error direction favours sensitivity over missed cases, but excess false positives remain an equity, anxiety and resource-use concern.",
     "Specificity becomes a design parameter that is set, sensitivity the measured, reported quantity, and Delaware's 0.356 is the discrimination problem of section 5.13.2 reappearing in the only place it can. On the external corpus the bootstrap gives specificity 0.774 [0.69, 0.80] against sensitivity 0.769 [0.44, 0.96] — the second loose because 26 impaired recordings cannot pin it down, the first tight because it is an in-sample quantity, the same 27 controls having defined the threshold and been scored against it. The prospective quantity has an exact, distribution-free answer: with the threshold at the k-th order statistic of n controls, prospective specificity follows a Beta(k, n+1−k) law, which for 27 controls gives 0.786 [0.62, 0.91], the number a clinician actually cares about, and Table (5.17) turns that law into the pilot's sample-size justification — a ±10-point interval needs about 59 healthy speakers, ±7.5 points about 108 (`results/reconstruction/control_threshold_precision.json`). The law assumes the control sample is drawn from the population the threshold will serve; a healthier-than-typical convenience sample sets the threshold too low and over-refers, an error direction that favours sensitivity over missed cases but leaves excess false positives as an equity, anxiety and resource-use concern.")
sub1("Band edges from the development distribution alone (tertiles at 0.334 and 0.533) produce an impairment rate that rises monotonically across bands in every cohort, which a binary flag cannot show, and band occupancy that differs sharply between cohorts — the external cohort's low band contains no impaired speaker at all, Pittsburgh's contains 21% — so banded reporting is the recommended primary presentation, with the reliability argument of section 5.14.1 behind it. Decision-curve analysis shows positive net benefit across the clinically relevant range in all three cohorts at their own prevalence, 36% to 56% impaired, which is not the deployment setting; recomputed at assumed community prevalences of 5%, 10% and 15% the curves remain positive but assume the operating characteristics transport, which section 5.13 shows they do not, so they are read as an upper bound [7]. None of this is deployed: it is the specified successor's operating rule, and the deployed instrument's threshold remains 0.367.",
     "Band edges from the development distribution alone (tertiles at 0.334 and 0.533) give an impairment rate that rises monotonically across bands in every cohort, which a binary flag cannot show, with band occupancy that differs sharply between cohorts — the external cohort's low band contains no impaired speaker, Pittsburgh's 21% — so banded reporting is the recommended primary presentation, with the reliability argument of section 5.14.1 behind it. Decision-curve analysis shows positive net benefit across the clinically relevant range in all three cohorts at their own prevalence, 36% to 56% impaired; recomputed at assumed community prevalences of 5%, 10% and 15% the curves remain positive but assume operating characteristics that section 5.13 shows do not transport, so they are read as an upper bound [7]. None of this is deployed: it is the specified successor's operating rule, and the deployed threshold remains 0.367.")

